# -----------------------------------------------------
# Monitors
# -----------------------------------------------------
# Keep in sync with ~/.config/hypr/monitors.conf (regenerated by nwg-displays).
#
# CONNECTOR NAMES ARE LOAD-BEARING AND NOT STABLE. They change when the driver
# changes AND when cables move ports. An output with no matching rule silently
# falls back to its EDID-preferred mode — on the AW3225QF that is 3840x2160@60,
# which is exactly how this config spent weeks stuck at 60Hz after the
# 2026-08-05 nvidia 610.43.03 -> 610.57.04 upgrade renamed everything.
#
# mango cannot match rules on make/model/serial (those exist only as IPC output
# fields, not monitorrule options), so the names below must be re-verified any
# time a cable moves or the driver updates. Check with:
#     which-port          (~/.local/bin/which-port)
#
# Current mapping, verified 2026-08-15:
#     DP-2      AW3225QF   4K240 gaming panel
#     DP-3      AW3423DWF  ultrawide
#     HDMI-A-2  AW2725DM   portrait
#
# GPU: ASUS RTX 4080 SUPER — 3x DP 1.4a + 2x HDMI 2.1, but only FOUR hardware
# heads, so at most four displays and high-pixel-clock modes can cost two heads
# each (see /usr/share/doc/nvidia/README, "MaxOneHardwareHead"). 4K240 needs DSC
# (2331.75 MHz pixel clock vs HBR3's ~25.92 Gbps) and is a 2-head candidate.
#
# rr = transform (0 none, 1 = 90° CCW). Never use negative x/y — XWayland
# mis-routes clicks on negative coordinates.

# Portrait panel, left of the stack (1440x2560 once rotated) — AW2725DM
monitorrule=name:^HDMI-A-2$,width:2560,height:1440,refresh:143.97,x:0,y:504,scale:1,rr:1,vrr:0,hdr:0

# Ultrawide (main) — AW3423DWF
# 164.9Hz = 1019.28 MHz pixel clock. Fallback if the 4K cannot hold 240:
#   refresh:99.98  (538.37 MHz)  or  refresh:59.97  (319.75 MHz)
monitorrule=name:^DP-3$,width:3440,height:1440,refresh:164.9,x:1440,y:0,scale:1,rr:0,vrr:0,hdr:0

# 4K240 below the ultrawide — the gaming panel. AW3225QF.
#
# scale is 1.3333333, matching the Hyprland config. wp_fractional_scale_v1
# transmits scale to clients as an integer number of 120ths, so 1.33 (159.6/120)
# is not representable: clients are told 160/120 = 1.333333 while mango lays the
# window out at 1.33. Client and compositor then disagree on the buffer size by a
# fraction of a pixel, which shows up as shimmering window edges — worst on small
# windows, where the error is a larger share of the window. 1.3333333 rounds to
# exactly 160/120, so both sides agree. Visually identical to 1.33 (0.25% apart).
# Other exactly-representable values if you ever want them: 1.25 (150/120),
# 1.5 (180/120) — any n/120.
monitorrule=name:^DP-2$,width:3840,height:2160,refresh:239.99,x:1440,y:1440,scale:1.3333333,rr:0,vrr:0,hdr:0

# --- Notes on the two Hyprland params with no direct equivalent -------------
# bitdepth,10 : mango exposes 10-bit only through the HDR path (hdr:1 on the
#   monitorrule + hdr_depth=2 + env=WLR_RENDERER,vulkan), and that path exists
#   only on the wl-only branch (scenefx has no vulkan renderer yet). Left off,
#   matching the Hyprland side where the HDR line is currently commented out.
# VRR : off deliberately — 3 mixed-refresh panels on NVIDIA black-screen when
#   the surface set changes. Flip vrr:1 on DP-2 alone for a gaming session.
